import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"

const html =
  '<p>Enrollment is KoAkademy&#39;s deepest subsystem. It has two cooperating layers: an <strong>operational pipeline</strong> that moves individual enrollments through verification, and a <strong>policy engine</strong> (&quot;Enrollment Blueprints&quot;) that decides the rules — who may enroll, what they must provide, what they pay, and who approves.</p>\n<h2>The operational pipeline</h2>\n<p><code>EnrollmentService</code> is the application façade. Every workflow mutation passes through <code>EnrollmentWorkflowCoordinator</code>, while <code>EnrollmentTransitionEngine</code> owns row locking, transactions, status and outcome projection, idempotency, and workflow events. A separate legacy adapter preserves enrollments created before policy activation.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Application</strong> — created by staff or through public online enrollment (when enabled).</li>\n<li><strong>Academic verification</strong> — registrar or department head verifies subjects, documents, and the computed assessment.</li>\n<li><strong>Cashier verification</strong> — payment is confirmed or recorded; the enrollment becomes official.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-enrollment</strong> — authorized transitions: undo, reopen, schedule-change notifications, class enrollment adjustments.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For policy enrollments, configured actions run in their listed order: entry-step actions during creation and target-step actions during transitions. An omitted action has no side effect. Every status change goes through the <strong>transition engine</strong> rather than direct edits, so the history of an enrollment is reconstructable. Assessment PDFs and notifications are queued only after the transaction commits (see <a href="/docs/system/queues-pdf">Queues &amp; PDF</a>).</p>\n<h3>Reading the execution map</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enrollment submitted</strong> is the trigger, not a staff approval. Public registration, staff registration, continuing enrollment, and API submissions enter through the coordinator. Channel rules determine which sources are allowed; scope and eligibility checks select and validate the published policy.</li>\n<li><strong>Status on entry</strong> describes the state already reached. Actions inside that stage run in numbered order when it is entered. Entry-stage actions run during submission, not when a department later approves.</li>\n<li><strong>Wait for staff action</strong> is a pause. Its roles/permission govern leaving that stage. The route label is the staff command; it is not automatic advancement.</li>\n<li><strong>Otherwise / default</strong> is the last route checked when no conditional route matches. Explicit route selection still checks that route&#39;s conditions. Academic/payment compatibility commands resolve eligible destinations under the same enrollment lock.</li>\n<li><strong>Final stage</strong> completes only after its actions and completion rules succeed. Failure rolls back status, linked payments, and other transactional writes; post-commit jobs are not dispatched.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Graph checks validate structure, not production readiness. Test Run evaluates policy rules and previews connected routes using the current sample facts. It does <strong>not</strong> execute payments, reserve seats, create PDFs, send emails, or prove later runtime payloads are valid.</p>\n<h3>Cancelling an enrollment</h3>\n<p>Cancellation is an administrative termination (<code>EnrollmentCancellationService</code>), not a workflow transition. It is available for both runtimes and for policies that never configured a cancellation stage, because an abandoned enrollment must always be stoppable.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authorized separately.</strong> It needs <code>Cancel:StudentEnrollment</code> (or super admin) and a written reason; both are stored on a <code>cancelled</code> workflow event. The action lives under <strong>More options → Cancel enrollment</strong> on the enrollment record, and the enrollment list can filter by outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>Terminal.</strong> <code>terminal_outcome</code> becomes <code>cancelled</code>, which stops every later transition, transition command, and compatibility verification. <strong>Undo cancellation</strong> (also needs <code>Reopen:StudentEnrollment</code>) reverses it: the status interrupted by the cancellation is restored and nothing else is touched.</li>\n<li><strong>Reverses only what the enrollment applied.</strong> Student synchronization applied by its own workflow events is restored from the audit snapshot (a snapshot that no longer matches the student&#39;s current state is treated as already reversed), and class seats reserved through its subject enrollments are released — except a seat another enrollment still points at, which belongs to both records. Subjects, tuition, and the reporting status are kept as history.</li>\n<li><strong>Never destroys money.</strong> Recorded enrollment payments stay linked and reported; the operator must confirm that they will refund separately, or reverse them first. Undoing a cancellation never reverses them. A completed policy enrollment must be reopened before it can be cancelled, and a cashier-verified legacy enrollment must be undone through the existing undo path first.</li>\n<li><strong>Reporting.</strong> <code>cancelled</code> is an outcome, not a reporting status, so the last real stage status is preserved and shown as &quot;was …&quot;. Enrolment analytics and student-directory population counts already exclude abandoned outcomes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Safe recovery after migration</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Capture an affected enrollment&#39;s <code>workflow_runtime</code>, <code>current_step_key</code>, snapshot ID, reporting status, and latest <code>transition_failed</code> reason. Do not replay actions or alter snapshots to diagnose it.</li>\n<li>Check the rollout banner. Saving a draft and publishing do not activate the engine. Deactivation affects future submissions only; existing policy enrollments still use their pinned snapshot.</li>\n<li>Compare the pinned snapshot with the active published version. The historical import can contain status actions only, while its compatibility draft contains explicit assignment, tuition, student synchronization, PDF, and notification actions. Publishing the draft does not repair older snapshots.</li>\n<li>Test a new enrollment on a staging copy through submission, academic review, payment, retry, and reopen. Verify linked transactions and student state, not only the canvas preview.</li>\n<li>For affected in-flight records, investigate and approve a record-specific repair separately. Never bulk replay billing or enrollment actions against production.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The enrollment list recognizes active published policies and existing policy-runtime records even without old pipeline settings. Rollout activation also persists a shared default so staff/public scopes first seen after activation inherit the intended runtime.</p>\n<h2>The policy engine (blueprints)</h2>\n<p>The engine lives in <code>app/Enrollment/</code> and is configured from the admin workspace (React UI under System Management → Enrollment Policy): blueprint shell, rule editor, workflow editor, and simulation reports.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>PolicyRegistry</code> / <code>PolicyManager</code></td>\n<td>Known policy types, presets, and CRUD over blueprints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PolicyResolver</code> / <code>PolicyInheritanceService</code></td>\n<td>Scope matching and inherit-and-override resolution (school → student type → program → period)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EnrollmentPolicyCompiler</code></td>\n<td>Compiles a published blueprint version into the executable policy the pipeline consults</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PolicySimulationService</code></td>\n<td>Dry-runs representative students against a draft, reporting blockers before publication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PolicyRolloutService</code></td>\n<td>Staged activation and rollback of published versions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EnrollmentTransitionEngine</code> / <code>EnrollmentWorkflowCoordinator</code></td>\n<td>Approval timelines, branches, and post-commit actions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EnrollmentCancellationService</code></td>\n<td>Authorized, audited cancellation of in-flight enrollments for both runtimes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategies &amp; handlers</td>\n<td>Assignment/billing strategies and integration action handlers (extension points)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Persistence: <code>EnrollmentPolicy</code> (the blueprint), <code>EnrollmentPolicyVersion</code> (immutable published versions), <code>EnrollmentPolicySnapshot</code> (what a given enrollment started with), and <code>EnrollmentWorkflowEvent</code> (the audit trail).</p>\n<p>Key invariants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Publication is immutable.</strong> A published version never changes; edits produce a new version.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapshots isolate in-flight enrollments.</strong> Activating a new version affects <em>future</em> enrollments only — active enrollments keep the snapshot they started with, and legacy enrollments stay legacy.</li>\n<li><strong>Simulation precedes activation.</strong> Operators are expected to resolve every simulated blocker before publishing.</li>\n<li><strong>Legacy behavior is isolated.</strong> Policy actions do not call legacy workflow methods, use ambient authentication, or open their own workflow transaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="doc-callout doc-callout-note">\n  <p class="doc-callout-label">About the DynamicEnrollmentPolicies flag</p><p><code>DynamicEnrollmentPolicies</code> defaults to <code>false</code>. While it is off, new enrollments remain on the legacy runtime. After an administrator explicitly activates it, future enrollments resolve a published blueprint and pin its compiled snapshot. Existing legacy and policy enrollments do not change runtime when the flag changes.</p>\n</div><h2>For operators vs. for developers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Operators</strong> configure blueprints without code — see the <strong>Enrollment Blueprints</strong> guides in the User Guide section (overview, quick start, scopes and inheritance, approvals, simulation and publication, troubleshooting).</li>\n<li><strong>Developers</strong> extend the engine through the documented contracts — rule handlers, action handlers, assignment/billing strategies, registry registration, and import envelopes. See <a href="/dev/enrollment-policy-extensions">Extending the enrollment engine</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Contributing rules of thumb</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Never mutate a published <code>EnrollmentPolicyVersion</code>; create a new version.</li>\n<li>Route status changes through the transition engine so workflow events stay complete.</li>\n<li>Route creation through <code>EnrollmentSubmissionData</code> and the coordinator so administrator, public, continuing, API, and Filament submissions resolve policies consistently.</li>\n<li>New rule/action types must be registered in the registry and covered by compiler and simulation tests.</li>\n<li>Keep the React blueprint editors in sync with the operator schema manifests the engine exposes.</li>\n</ul>\n'
